static inline void crc_init_x86(AVCRC *ctx, int le, int bits, uint32_t poly, int ctx_size)
{
    uint64_t poly_;
    if (le) {
        // convert the reversed representation to regular form
        poly = reverse(poly, bits) >> 1;
    }
    // convert to 32 degree polynomial
    poly_ = ((uint64_t)poly) << (32 - bits);
    uint64_t div;
    uint8_t *dst = (uint8_t*)(ctx + 1);
    if (le) {
        ctx[0] = CLMUL_LE;
        AV_WN64(dst,      xnmodp(4 * 128 - 32, poly_, 32, &div, le));
        AV_WN64(dst +  8, xnmodp(4 * 128 + 32, poly_, 32, &div, le));
        uint64_t tmp = xnmodp(128 - 32, poly_, 32, &div, le);
        AV_WN64(dst + 16, tmp);
        AV_WN64(dst + 24, xnmodp(128 + 32, poly_, 32, &div, le));
        AV_WN64(dst + 32, tmp);
        AV_WN64(dst + 40, xnmodp(64, poly_, 32, &div, le));
        AV_WN64(dst + 48, div);
        AV_WN64(dst + 56, reverse(poly_ | (1ULL << 32), 32));
    } else {
        ctx[0] = CLMUL_BE;
        AV_WN64(dst,      xnmodp(4 * 128 + 64, poly_, 32, &div, le));
        AV_WN64(dst +  8, xnmodp(4 * 128, poly_, 32, &div, le));
        AV_WN64(dst + 16, xnmodp(128 + 64, poly_, 32, &div, le));
        AV_WN64(dst + 24, xnmodp(128, poly_, 32, &div, le));
        AV_WN64(dst + 32, xnmodp(64, poly_, 32, &div, le));
        AV_WN64(dst + 48, div);
        AV_WN64(dst + 40, xnmodp(96, poly_, 32, &div, le));
        AV_WN64(dst + 56, poly_ | (1ULL << 32));
# Crypto wave 0 / batch 0: AVAES and AVAESCTR
Route verified: `type`; objective: `wrap`. Both public headers publish only
forward declarations. There are no public fields to wrap. Private storage stays
C-owned; the `ffibox` layout names the opaque FFI pointee, not a Rust-sized body.
Construction policies call the C allocators for the full aligned private bodies.
No shim or compiled C changed. No `Send`, `Sync`, or clone implementation added.
## Analysis and surface
Read aes.h, aes_internal.h, aes.c, x86/aes_init.c, aes_ctr.h/c, the
Wavefront records/dependency closure, and libavformat holders (crypto, MOV,
MOV CENC, SRTP, HLS/sample encryption, MXF). There were no Rust AES consumers.
Submitted both type records and all five constructor/initializer/free lifecycle
records through Wavefront `--update`.
AES stores only inline schedules/scratch and a process-lifetime C/assembly
function pointer. CTR stores inline AES, counters and offset, with no pointer
fields. Keys are copied, never retained. Contexts have exclusive holders; AES
holders free with av_free/av_freep, CTR holders with av_aes_ctr_free (av_free).
Neither type has a C cloning routine or field disposer. Borrowed handles are
provided by ffibox; their pointer seams stay crate-private.
AesFree delegates release to the existing Heap policy; AesCtrFree binds the C
free routine. Both release construction-phase storage without reading it. Safe
constructors promote storage only after initialization. Reinitialization requires
an exclusive handle. AES rejects invalid key lengths *before* FFI because C
otherwise changes rounds/crypt before returning EINVAL. CTR uses a fixed 16-byte
key and resets counter/partial-block state. Processing/IV symbols remain for
the subsequent symbol batch; equivalence tests use raw C observers only to
inspect states established by the safe lifecycle API.
## Validation
Reusable builds: `/target/crustify/builds/{plain,asan-ubsan,tsan,bsan,coverage}`.
`build.json` equals the orchestrator's version 3 specification. C source trees
match 1019f8f036, including AES/CTR and their dependencies. Checked each variant's
FFMPEG_CONFIGURATION/CC_IDENT and instrumentation: gcc 14.2 coverage, clang 20.1.2
ASan/UBSan and TSan, and the matched BSan clang 23.1.3/plugin. Builds were not
modified. GCOV_PREFIX and LLVM_PROFILE_FILE redirect coverage to private outputs;
coverage was inspected with gcov 14.2 and nightly llvm-cov. No reusable batch
runner was supplied; commands below use the prepared libraries directly.
Agent-unique logs, targets, profiles and line coverage:
`/tmp/crustify-crypto-w0-b0/`.
- `cargo check --workspace`: passes.
- `cargo clippy --workspace`: passes. The additional strict all-target gate
  passed before rebasing onto SHA; after that rebase it flags only two
  unrelated manual_c_str_literals in SHA/SHA512 equivalence tests, introduced
  by the sibling batch. Those files are outside this worklist and unchanged.
- libavutil-sys regenerated by its build script; check and tests pass.
- `crustify scan-unsafe crustify/rust/ffmpeg --json --sites
  unsafe_fns_pub_smell unsafe_fns_priv_smell raw_ptr_pub_smell raw_ptr_priv_smell
  ref_to_type_wrapper_smell`: no target unsafe/raw smells; no undocumented AES
  sites. Raw FFI is confined to construction/release and small lifecycle calls.
- Eight inline unit tests, four safe UB integration tests, two lifecycle
  equivalence integration tests, and four compile-fail doctests. No new public
  unsafe API beyond ffibox's inherited seams, so no new ub_unsafe suite.
- ASan/UBSan: workset passes; workspace runtime tests pass (excluding the existing
  diagnostic-snapshot gate). Rust address instrumentation and the sanitized C
  libraries were used, leak detection enabled, UBSan halt_on_error enabled.
- TSan: all workset runtime tests pass. `aes::parallel_constructors` also runs
  alone in a fresh test process to exercise simultaneous first initialization
  of C's process-wide lookup tables. Owners are created/dropped within each
  thread, never transferred between threads.
- BSan: only exact tests `aes::lifecycle_reborrows` and
  `aes_ctr::lifecycle_reborrows`, each under timeout 900, pass. No entire suite
  or binary was run under BSan.
- Miri: four Rust-only validation/null-adoption/layout tests pass. Foreign-call
  unit tests are cfg(not(miri)); Miri never executes a C routine.
